{% block guide_content %}
<p class="guide-lead">A baby guessing game is one of the easiest baby shower activities to run: every guest predicts when the baby will arrive, how much they will weigh, whether it is a boy or a girl, and sometimes even the name. Here is how to turn it into a real shared moment, in person or from afar.</p>

<h2>Why a guessing game works so well</h2>
<p>It involves everyone without asking anything of anyone: no supplies to buy, no complicated rules, and everybody plays at their own pace. Best of all, it creates a thread that runs until the birth. The party lasts a few hours, but the predictions stay open, so your guests wait for the result with you, sometimes for weeks.</p>

<h2>The questions to ask</h2>
<p>Five categories are plenty, and you can switch on only a few of them:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Gender</strong>: boy or girl, the all-time classic.</li>
    <li><strong>Name</strong>: as a free answer, or in "hints mode", where each guest gets three tries and a hint after each one (length of the name, first letter and so on).</li>
    <li><strong>Date and time</strong> of the birth.</li>
    <li><strong>Weight</strong> at birth.</li>
    <li><strong>Length</strong> at birth.</li>
</ul>
<p>The fewer the questions, the more guests answer: the form takes under a minute to fill in. For more ideas on questions and rules, read our guide to the <a href="{{ path('app_guide', {'_locale': 'en', 'slug': 'baby-prediction-game'}) }}">baby prediction game</a>.</p>

<h2>Paper cards or an online game?</h2>
<p>Paper cards have their charm, but they have to be passed around, the answers have to be typed up again, and above all they leave out the relatives who cannot attend. An online game is shared with a simple link (family group chat, invitation, text message): everyone plays from their phone, wherever they are, without creating an account. All the answers are gathered in one place and ranked automatically at the reveal.</p>

<h2>Set up your game in 4 steps</h2>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Choose your categories</strong> and, if you like, add a title and a photo (an ultrasound picture, for example).</li>
    <li><strong>Share the link</strong> in your family group chat or on the baby shower invitation.</li>
    <li><strong>Let your guests make their predictions</strong>, before or during the party. They can also leave a short message for the parents-to-be.</li>
    <li><strong>After the birth, enter the real answers</strong>: the ranking is calculated for you, and you can notify by email the guests who left their address.</li>
</ol>

<h2>How to pick the winners</h2>
<p>For gender and name, everyone who guessed right wins. For date, weight and length, the three closest predictions make the podium. A small symbolic prize is enough: a slice of cake, a baby gift, or simply family glory.</p>

<div class="guide-tip">
    <p><strong>Tip:</strong> decide in advance when predictions close (the day of the party, or at the birth). You can close the game at any time so nobody cheats by already knowing the answer.</p>
</div>

<h2>What about relatives who live far away?</h2>
<p>This is where an online game makes the difference: grandparents abroad, childhood friends, colleagues… everyone plays from their phone, in their own language (the site is available in six languages), and discovers the reveal at the same time as the guests in the room. For a virtual baby shower, it is often the one game that works as well remotely as in person.</p>

<p>If your party also announces the baby's gender, take a look at our ideas for an <a href="{{ path('app_guide', {'_locale': 'en', 'slug': 'gender-reveal-guessing-game'}) }}">interactive gender reveal</a>.</p>
